    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d696">

                <pb facs="0742" n="484" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg484"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 300.115/4322</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-07-22T19:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 22, 1915, 7 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 7.35 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2511. Your 1852, July 15.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d696-f1"/>
                    Sir Edward Grey has reminded me that no
                    court has yet passed on the validity of the order in council of March 11, and he
                    intimated that, the prize court in the case of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Neches</hi> would probably have to pass on this question. The foregoing was
                    an incidental part of a general conversation on the validity of the order in
                    council. He promises an early reply to your 1852.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
